# A HOLE MAY OPEN WITH A BRACE, AND THAT IS WHY `{{` IS NOT FREE.
#
# This is the migration proof for the ruling in `docs/text-law.md`, mechanized.
#
# Two standing laws meet here. `law.brace` binds "braces carry structured packs,
# descriptor homes and descriptor application". `law.literal.text` binds
# "interpolation preserves literal and EXPRESSION segments". A structured pack is
# an expression, so a hole may open with `{` — and therefore `{{` is ALREADY
# spoken for. It is the spelling of "a hole whose expression begins with a pack".
#
# Python, C#, Rust, Zig and C++ `std::format` all spend `{{` on a literal brace,
# and that is the strongest familiarity argument available. This file is the
# reason the ruling declines it anyway: adopting `{{` would change the answer of
# every line below from a computed number to its own source text, and no
# familiar spelling is worth a language that cannot apply a relation to a pack
# inside a hole.
#
# THIS FILE IS A SHRINK-ONLY OBLIGATION. It must keep answering 3. If a later
# lane implements `{{` as an escape, the first line stops saying 3 and this
# oracle is what says so.
# expect: n=3 spaced=3 v=7 pack=3
# @corpus current

# NO SPACE between the hole opener and the pack. This is the exact collision.
print("n={{10, 20, 30}:len()}")

# The spaced form is the same expression and must give the same answer; if the
# two ever diverge, whitespace has become semantic inside a hole.
print("spaced={ {10, 20, 30}:len() }")

x = 7
print("v={x}")

# A pack that is not a literal is the same shape one step removed, so the fact
# does not rest on a single parse path.
n = {1, 2, 3}:len()
print("pack={n}")
